  "reactionVersion": 1,
  "mechanismSummary": "List, Lerner and Barbas (2000) showed that the amino acid proline catalyses a direct asymmetric aldol between unmodified acetone and an aldehyde. Proline's amine condenses with acetone: it adds while the CO₂H protonates the oxygen, the carboxylate shuttles the N–H proton, and the acid helps water leave. The carboxylate then removes an α-proton to give the enamine. The enamine attacks 4-nitrobenzaldehyde while the CO₂H delivers its proton to the aldehyde oxygen (the Houk–List transition state); this step sets the stereocentre, giving the (R)-aldol in 76 % ee. Hydrolysis runs the condensation backwards and frees proline. Like an aldolase enzyme (class I, through an enamine of a lysine), proline uses an amine and an acid together. The app does not check stereochemistry yet, so the drawings carry no wedges and either face is accepted.",
